{
  "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
  "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Donald B. Verrilli, Jr., on behalf of the Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Kentucky's lethal injection procedures pose a danger of cruelly inhumane executions. If the first drug in the three-drug sequence, the anesthetic thiopental, is not effectively administered to the executed inmate, then the second drug, pancuronium, will induce a terrifying conscious paralysis and suffocation and the third drug, potassium chloride, will inflict an excruciating burning pain as it courses through the veins."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Donald B. Verrilli, Jr., on behalf of the Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "and, beyond that, the protocol's -- and I think this is critically important -- the protocol's procedures for monitoring to assure that the inmate is adequately anesthetized are practically nonexistent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Who would be a medical doctor -- and medical doctors, according to the Code of Ethics of the American Medical Association, can't participate."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Donald B. Verrilli, Jr., on behalf of the Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, Your Honor, of course, that's why there is another practical alternative here, which solves that problem, which is the single dose of barbituate, which does not require the participation of a medically trained professional."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Verrilli",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. VERRILLI: We don't make the argument that States are required to choose the least painful method possible. Our standard is grounded on three, I think, extremely solid, well- established points of Eighth Amendment doctrine."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: But do you also agree with the counter- proposition that if it is not properly administered there is some risk of excruciating pain?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: I take it this is obvious, but I wondered when I went through the brief. I assume this phlebotomist is not an MD?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Englert",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. ENGLERT: That's -- to say they leave the room is accurate, but the suggestion that they have no further part is misleading. They go into the next room. They watch through a one-way mirror, carefully watching to make sure nothing has gone wrong."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: So the nub of your argument\nreally is they have not made a case or they do not have\na record case for any significant likelihood of\nexcruciating pain is this."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: It's still unclear why\nthey should make such an effort to get trained personnel\nin the first instance and then even if they are in the\nnext room, why isn't, why did they deliberately pick\nnonprofessional people to both administer the drugs and\nto check the inmate for consciousness."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Human Rights Watch",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0002-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "With respect to the time it takes to carry\nout an execution and whether that's a legitimate\nconsideration, I actually invite the Court's attention\nto one of the briefs, amicus briefs, filed in support of\nPetitioners, the Human Rights Watch brief, which in turn\ncites the decision of the UN Human Rights Committee in\nthe NG case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Amicus Curiae)",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0002-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF GREGORY G. GARRE,\nON BEHALF OF THE UNITED STATES,\nAS AMICUS CURIAE,\nSUPPORTING THE RESPONDENTS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0003-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "seven were inadequately -- the experts in that case admitted it was likely that one was not likely an at the time -- at the time that the pancuronium and potassium were put in the system similarly in the case in North Carolina the evidence credited by the court was that with respect to four of them the condemned inmate was on the -- any gasping struggling not the kind of involuntary twitching that Mr. Englert is worried about but clear that the anesthetics are not working with respect to the lethality of thiopental at page 42 of the joint appendix Dr. Heath says that thiopental will be lethal by itself three grams at page 494 he says indeed it will be lethal by itself in virtually every case at two grams. At page, at page forgive me I don't have the page number reference handy but Dr. Dershowitz the state's expert says the same thing now the reference that Mr. Englert referred to at page 499 is where Dr. Heath is being a asked a question of whether would you expect death to occur when three grams are administered but he is being asked a series of questions about administration in a surgical procedure which are using ventilators and other procedures to keep the person alive and he said in that setting the answer is no so that's just not a fair representation of the record at all now with respect to the question of"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Verrilli",
      "case_id": "2007-07-5439",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6e0f44b202619504b34435027801837d54f3145dcfa813c91e97e9924b862157",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6e0f44b20261-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "whether we ought to analogize this to the deliberate\nindifference standard and convictions of confinement\ncases it seems to me there is a fundamental difference\nhere which is that the Commonwealth of Kentucky is\nmaking a deliberate choice here."
    },
